findings.body:
Any authenticated caller can supply `body.accountId` and load that account. The handler never checks that the caller is support staff or is otherwise authorized for the target id — the comment is the only "control".

**Failure mode:** `POST/GET` with a valid session and `{"accountId":"victim-id"}` returns the victim account JSON. Before this change, lookup was always scoped to `caller.accountId`.

**Fix:** only honor an explicit id after an authorization check (support/admin role or equivalent); otherwise always use `caller.accountId`. Example shape:

```typescript
const body = await req.json().catch(() => ({}));
const id =
  body.accountId && caller.isSupport
    ? body.accountId
    : caller.accountId;
if (body.accountId && !caller.isSupport) {
  return Response.json({ error: "forbidden" }, { status: 403 });
}
```

Confirm the real staff claim on your `auth()` return type before copying the field name.
